rsity he only stuck  around at harvard for one year though as  he would transfer to mit during his  second year morris would get his  bachelor's in mechanical engineering in  1952 and he would instantly turn around  and get his masters as well in 1953.  morris also pursued a doctoral degree  from mit but he would eventually quit in  1955 after his thesis was rejected  multiple times  given that morris already had a  bachelor's and a master's in engineering  though it wasn't that hard for him to  find a job  in 1955 morris joined sylvania  semiconductor and he would work there  for three years before switching over to  texas instruments and this is where he  would spend the majority of his working  days unlike many entrepreneurs who  started business during college or after  a couple of years of work morris sells  his job until it was basically time to  retire he slowly worked his way up the  corporate ladder and stayed at texas  instruments for 25 years  in 1961 texas instruments sponsored  morris's phd in electrical engineering  from stanford and i think this is what  really got morris to stick around  after receiving his phd morris got  promoted to vice president and  eventually president of ti  it wasn't until 1983 that morris left ti  when he was offered a job as ceo at  general instrument corporation he only  stuck around for a year though before he  quit in 1985.  at this point morris was 55 years old  and i'm sure he made plenty of money as  an engineer and executive so he could  have easily retired but instead he  decided to partner with taiwan and  phillips and found tsmc in 1987.  by the time tsmc was created though  legacy players like intel ti and nec  dominated the market and given that it  took years to develop and manufacture  advanced semiconductors the barrier to  enter the industry was quite high  but on the bright side if you were able  to cross that barrier you had a great  shot as band value doesn't mean that  much in this industry after all  consumers don't know or care about where  these chips come from so electronics  companies just choose the most cost  effective supplier tsmc's first chips  were two process nodes or basically two  generations behind the competition but  morris was able to make this work by  pricing the semiconductors ahead of the  cost curve in other words morris took a  loss at the beginning in order to  attract customers and he leveraged  economies of skill to turn a profit  while simple in theory this idea was  extremely controversial in the 1980s as  it was extremely difficult to reach said  economies of scale  so morris was making a massive gamble  with the strategy but given that he was  backed by taiwan and phillips he had  some pretty deep pockets  within four to five years morris was  able to reduce the gap between tsmc and  the legacy players to just one process  node and around the same time morris  would take tsmc public in 1993 through  the taiwan stock exchange  four years later tsmc would become the  first taiwanese company to be listed on  the new york stock exchange  with the help of the public markets and  the irrational.com bubble morris was  able to continue his super aggressive  strategy throughout the 1990s and tsmc  would catch up to the legacy players by  the end of the century  tsmc had finally broken into the  semiconductor industry and they could  have taken it easy by simply keeping up  with the competition but maurice had no  plans of slowing down thus far he had  dedicated the company's efforts into  catching up to the competition and now  he was going to pour in all of their  effort into becoming the global leader  given that he was over 70 years old  though morris would hand over the  position of ceo to rick sai  under rick's leadership tsmc continued  to rapidly innovate their chips in fact  between 2006 and 2008 rickovers are the  company's biggest sleeping manufacturing  from 65 nanometer chips to 40 nanometer  chips but despite this fast progress  tsmc didn't grow all that much during  this time period as the global ship  industry was experiencing a massive  recession following the dot-com bubble  and it didn't take long for the 2008  financial recession to roll around but  morris actually saw great opportunity  through this recession he witnessed the  emergence of mobile technology like  smartphones and tablets and he was  extremely excited to be part of this  revolution  in fact morris was so excited and he saw  so much opportunity that he would return  as ceo in 2009 at age 78. it looks like  morris's primary goal with returning to  tsmc was to make a deal with apple and  in 2011 this would become a reality as  apple agreed to begin trial production  of the a5 and a6 chips with the dsmc  from there it didn't take long for apple  to commit to working with the tsmc for  the long term and today apple accounts  for 25 of tsmc's entire revenue  anyway after the apple partnership was  solidified orris announced that he was  going to retire in 2013 and make rick  ceo again but when 2013 actually came  around morris decided to stick around  for even longer not only was the mobile  space exploding but nvidia and amd were  about to go on their own exponential  runs  nvidia started embracing the machine  learning and ai industries in the  mid-2010s as the pc market started to  stagnate  in the meantime amd was basically  employing morris's strategy of pricing  chips ahead of the cost curve with ryzen  and this turned out to be a massive  success and revitalized the pc industry  to make things even better bitcoin  exploded in 2017 which resulted in crazy  high demand for nvidia and amd gpus for  crypto mining rigs and given that tsmc  is the supplier for nvidia apple amd and  everyone else you can think of this boom  directly carried over to tsmc  tsmc managed to breakthrough.com highs  in late 2016 and profits start to grow  exponentially once again  and with that morris would finally  retire in june of 2018 at age 86.  after morris left mark lew served as  co-ceo became ceo and chairman over the  next few years though tsmc started to  stagnate once again  and though this makes mark look bad it's  really not his fault morris is just  really good at leaving at the peak  anyway though the company's goals slow  down for a few years once a pandemic and  supply shortage hit tsmc would go  parabolic once again  however the supply shortage has not been  all good for tsmc  sure their profits and stock prices have  gone to the moon but the shortage has  also drawn a lot of attention towards  tsmc's monopoly basically the entire  chip shortage circles back to tsmc and  it's not particularly a good thing that  one company has so much influence so  many governments around the world have  been trying to diversify chip production  for example former president donald  trump pressured tsmc into building a 12  billion manufacturing plant in phoenix  similarly tsmc agreed to open a research  and development facility in japan  in the meantime the european union is  trying to beat tsmc to two nanometer  production but this is going to be  extremely difficult tsmc themselves are  only on three nanometer production and  they've never slowed down their  innovation momentum  according to peter hanbury if you want  to compete against dsmc onto an animator  you're going to have to spend 15 billion  this year then 18 billion next year and  then 20 billion dollars a year after  that and that's just three nanometers  the european union wants the btsmc to  two nanometers the problem is that as  technology continues to improve it  becomes exponentially harder and more  expensive to take the next step so it's  basically impossible to just step in and  compete against tsmc you would likely  need a budget of 100 billion dollars to  beat tsmc to 2 nanometers in a short  period of time and though the eu can  technically afford that it's unlikely  that they'll actually make such a large  investment as more time goes by though  it'll just become even harder to  challenge tsmc so maybe that you will  bite the bullet but we'll just have to  wait and see  while tsmc is no doubt a monopoly it  could be argued that tsmc has thus far  been an ethical monopoly they haven't  voluntarily jacked up prices or slowed  down innovation or been involved in any  sort of corruption even though all of  these would have been super easy given  their influence and control tsmc is  actually extremely strict about ethics  and integrity just a few months ago they  fired seven employees for leaking  information about apple's upcoming chips  since morris founded tsmc the company  has centered around four core values  which include integrity commitment  innovation and customer trust  while these sounds super cliche tsmc's  history over the past 34 years has shown  that tsmc has indeed upheld these values  i mean just take a look at how nvidia  ceo jensen huang talks about tsmc  because i think that moore's  his career  his philosophies  tsmc  its strategies  its core values  is absolutely  a study  in industrial revolution  there is no question about it hearing  that i think it's pretty clear that the  world's biggest companies don't work  with dsmc because they have to but  because they want to and as long as  that's the case maybe the tsmc monopoly  isn't all that bad do you guys think  that something has to be done about tsmc  comment that down below also drop like  if you're grateful for tsmc's chips and  of course consider joining our discord  community to suggest future video ideas  and consider subscribing and see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm hari and i'll see you guys on  the next one  you

